 "standard_code": "KS3: Number",
 "standard_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/national-curriculum-in-england-mathematics-programmes-of-study",
 "standard_text": "Use integer powers and associated real roots (square, cube and higher), recognise powers of 2, 3, 4, 5; simplify and manipulate algebraic expressions to maintain equivalence.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 8 laws of indices worksheets: multiply and divide powers, power of a power, zero and negative indices, and simplify algebraic terms like 3x² × 4x⁵. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Same base: multiply → add the indices, divide → subtract, power of a power → multiply.",
 "key_sub": "a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1 ÷ aⁿ. Multiply or divide the numbers in front as normal.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>The <b>index</b> (power) tells you how many times the base is multiplied by itself. With the <b>same base</b>:</p><ul><li>Multiplying: add the indices. <i>a</i><sup>m</sup> × <i>a</i><sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>m+n</sup></li><li>Dividing: subtract the indices. <i>a</i><sup>m</sup> ÷ <i>a</i><sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>m−n</sup></li><li>Power of a power: multiply the indices. (<i>a</i><sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>mn</sup></li><li><i>a</i><sup>0</sup> = 1 and <i>a</i><sup>−n</sup> = 1 ÷ <i>a</i><sup>n</sup></li></ul><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify 3<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> × 4<i>x</i><sup>5</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply the numbers: 3 × 4 = 12.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Add the indices: 2 + 5 = 7.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 12<i>x</i><sup>7</sup></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Simplify (2<i>a</i><sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<sup>2</sup> = 4.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>(<i>a</i><sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>6</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 4<i>a</i><sup>6</sup></p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Work out 2<sup>−3</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>1 ÷ 2<sup>3</sup> = 1 ÷ 8.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <span class='fr'><span class='fn'>1</span><span class='fd'>8</span></span></p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying the indices when you multiply powers: <i>a</i><sup>3</sup> × <i>a</i><sup>4</sup> = <i>a</i><sup>7</sup>, not <i>a</i><sup>12</sup>.</li><li>Multiplying the bases: 3<sup>4</sup> × 3<sup>2</sup> = 3<sup>6</sup>, not 9<sup>6</sup>.</li><li>Thinking 5<sup>0</sup> = 0. It is 1.</li><li>Thinking 2<sup>−3</sup> is negative. It is 1/8.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What are the laws of indices?",
   "a": "With the same base, add indices when multiplying, subtract when dividing, and multiply when raising a power to a power. a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is any number to the power 0 equal to 1?",
   "a": "Because aⁿ ÷ aⁿ = a⁰, and any number divided by itself is 1."
  },
  {
   "q": "Does a negative index make a number negative?",
   "a": "No. It makes a fraction: 2⁻³ = 1/8."
  },
  {
   "q": "How do I simplify 3x² × 4x⁵?",
   "a": "Multiply the numbers (3 × 4 = 12) and add the indices of x (2 + 5 = 7) to get 12x⁷."
  }
 ],
